[Music]  hi I'm Toby and welcome back to another  episode of The Joy of mathematics and so  glad you could join me again today are  you ready to work on some mathematical  artwork because that's what I thought we  would do today no stress no equations  just relax  and gain an appreciation of our  mathematical world I'm going to run some  ideas across the bottom of the screen  that we will come to understand today I  would also like to thank today's sponsor  brilliant org so I'm going to start off  my artwork by splitting it in two  I'm going to have a line that separates  the sky from the grass we're going to  get a beautiful blue sky by filling it  in like so this is my small strokes  there all right so in the sky don't  forget to add some sunlight and we can  also add some little birds in the corner  here all right so we're also going to  need to do the grass so grab agree and  just like we did the sky fill in a nice  green healthy grass here if you can see  the backdrop behind me today the grass  isn't so green that's a little dry it's  also a little smoky from some of the  smoke haze from bush fires we've got  going on  all right so we've set up our scene now  what I'm going to do is I'm going to  place a little mushroom right here in  the middle of the grass it's the kind of  mushroom that would attract fairies we  may be your mathematicians today but  we're not too serious that we can't  include a little bit of magic so what I  want to do next is I want to draw a hill  that the mushroom is going to be on but  just so that everything looks nice and  aesthetic I don't want the hill to be  too close to the horizon and I don't  want it to be too close to the mushroom  either in fact right in the middle of  these two would be perfect and that's  what we're going to do we're going to  measure out the distance here find  halfway and that is going to be the tip  of our Hill I'm just going to switch us  to our little chalk pen here so it's a  bit easier to see on the background of  the chalk all right so there's the tip  of our Hill now I thought about using a  ruler to do this I even bought a ruler  along with me but then I thought at the  last minute that might be a bit of  overkill we can just measure this using  our minds and our brains so if we have a  look here we want the hill to curve down  we want it to be equally far away from  the horizon and the mushroom so what  would be equidistant about there and  about there I think that would be the  next point of our Hill and the same on  the other side if we measure with our  eyes down from here the same distance  from the horizon as the mushroom we  might get there in continuing that  little measurement with our eyes all the  way around we start to see what this  hill is going to look like  not everything needs to be done  perfectly with a ruler I think maybe  that some of the fun of it is getting it  maybe slightly wrong but we know the  idea we're going for I'm going to go  ahead and add some happy little trees to  our beautiful Hill in here not too many  we don't want to go overboard but these  are some happy little pine cones that  one didn't quite make it to the zoo pine  cones are some of my favorite trees to  draw who actually got a cone sitting up  here on my easel today to celebrate the  festive season I considered wearing it  on my own head but I didn't want to mess  up my hair  you see cones are interesting shapes  because they contain other shapes inside  of them let me show you what I mean I've  got some scissors here and I'm going to  make a few cuts so I think you can tell  that because of the shape of the cone if  I was to cut it straight across I would  get a circle but if I was to cut it a  little bit wonky across here it's a  little bit less obvious but I would get  an ellipse a kind of stretched out  circle let's see if we can get one it's  a little bit jagged but you can see  there I've cut on a wonky angle and I've  got an ellipse out and now what I'm  going to do is cut this one more way and  that is going to be parallel to one of  the sides so this is the side that I  want to be parallel to I'm going to make  a cut across here like this and we'll  see what shape we get so here we go  so I've tried my best there to cut this  side parallel and this is the shape that  we've ended up with it may shock you -  no but this shape here is actually  exactly the same shape as our hill that  we drew before that's now two different  places that we've found this shape  hiding let's see if I trace it out this  is a little precarious but that's the  fun of it so I'm just tracing around the  shape that we cut out from our cone to  see what it looks like on our artwork  all right moment of truth there we go  yeah that looks about right looks quite  similar to our Hill now this shape has a  name and it is called a parabola now  we've found parabolas already in a  couple of places but they actually occur  everywhere in nature as well as in many  man-made things here's some footage of a  couple of other parabolas that I found  around town there's a parabola in the  curve of some dishes at the physics  department and in the falling water of a  fountain near the library a water  fountain or any object thrown up into  the air at an angle will trace out a  parabola the object retains its  horizontal motion if we neglect air  resistance and so the only force acting  on it is gravity causing a downward  acceleration like many of nature's  secrets humans have taken inspiration  from the parabolas one useful property  that the parabolas have is that they  reflect all parallel light rates to a  focus  a mushroom was the focus of our last  parabola in here but if this up here is  a big dish then we could put some  electronics here in the focus or in the  case of the one I showed you before from  the physics department you could maybe  heat up some water in here as a  demonstration of some solar heating it  works both ways a torch and headlights  on a car would have a parabolic mirror  with the bulb in the middle light from  the bulb would be reflected off the  parabolic mirror out into a beam of  light so let's just add a little bit  more color to our drawing here you're  basically done we can add in a few more  parabolic Hills don't go overboard just  keep it nice and pretty I hope that this  video has been a little bit of  inspiration for you to keep an eye out  for mathematical shapes all around you  if you find a way to work some parabolas  into an artwork of your own then send it  to me and I'll share my favorite ones on  Twitter it is now time to thank our  sponsor brilliant org you can help to  spread the love of math to people you  know by gifting them a brilliant premium  subscription it is a way to nurture  curiosity build confidence and develop  problem-solving skills brilliant content  breaks down complexities and to  understandable bite-sized chunks that  will guide someone from curiosity to  mastery head along to brilliant org  slash  tibbie's and grab a gift subscription to  help someone that you love spark a  lifelong love of learning I would like  to wish you happy studying and I hope  that you have an absolutely mathematical  day  a special thanks to all of my patrons  including Helmholtz our first patron  head of the day submitted by Rob  [Music]  you

